The Material Protection and Transferability Problem

A Formal Framework for Protected Institutional Experimentation, Reversibility, and Evidence-Governed Scaling

Research group: Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

Document type: Theoretical working paper (dissertation chapter 1, revised)

Version 0.4 — formal corrections, clarified limits, and prospective calibration protocol

Status: Theoretical programme. No experiments, physical tests, or coding training have been conducted. P1–P2 are proved under stated assumptions; P1.4 is conditional on an explicit monotonicity assumption; P3 is a decomposition with conditional bounds, not an unconditional bound; P4 is a restricted budget theorem and an open problem. Empirical claims are prospective.

Abstract

Version 0.4 incorporates the formal and bibliographic corrections identified in the audit of version 0.3. The main changes are: (i) Definition 4 is restated with normalized scores in [0,1], replacing the undefined minimum of incommensurable objects; (ii) P1.4 is restated with an explicit monotonicity assumption on the cross-derivative of log participation probability, and the S-admissibility condition for transport is separated from the signability condition for the selection term; (iii) the distinction between study participation and treatment compliance is made explicit; (iv) the counting of design levers in P2.2 is corrected from three to four, and the failure-time formula is bounded below by zero with the zero-denominator case handled separately; (v) the portability notation is unified across Definitions 6 and 10; (vi) P3 is renamed a decomposition, δ_site is defined as a conditional residual rather than a population divergence, and Λ is distinguished from the Ben-David joint ideal error; (vii) the Vivalt attribution in F1 is removed and the numerical anchors are presented as provisional protocol conventions; (viii) all layer thresholds are declared conventions requiring a justification memo; (ix) the calibration protocol distinguishes administrative from survey attrition, and the Stockton case is specified with exact figures. No empirical results are reported. The framework's contribution is a reporting discipline that makes protection-related transfer failures visible, not a theorem that guarantees successful transfer.

Keywords: institutional experimentation, external validity, transportability, reversibility, experimentalist governance, protected spaces, transferability, partial identification

