Part I — Foundations

1. Problem statement

Let Ω be a target environment in which a proposed institutional rule $r \in \mathcal{R}$ would operate. Let Π be an experimental protocol exposing participants to $r$ under design conditions. The question is whether evidence from Π supports inferences about $r$ in Ω, and how the design of Π — particularly its protection regime — affects that inference.

The literature theorizes parts of this problem. Experimentalist governance describes recursive rule revision through local experimentation and peer review. Strategic niche management theorizes protected spaces, but protection there is directed at the innovation, not at the persons experimenting; Smith and Raven distinguish shielding, nurturing, and empowerment, and note that empowerment is the least developed of the three. Regulatory sandboxes integrate protections, but these are oriented toward consumers and third parties rather than participants' material security, and critical assessment finds that sandbox learnings often fail to translate into broader regulatory improvements. Policy transfer theory identifies uninformed, incomplete, and inappropriate transfer as distinct failure modes. The Declaration of Helsinki requires post-trial access provisions to be arranged in advance, which is a close ethical analogue to transferable protection but not a design variable for external validity. Cartwright and Hardie's support factors are what must be present for a policy to work in a new setting; transferable protection is arguably one such factor, but the framework does not integrate protection into an inferential decomposition.

No tradition examined integrates these dimensions into an auditable design variable for external-validity inference with explicit identification conditions and a falsifiable rejection rule. This paper proposes that integration, while being explicit about what the integration can and cannot establish.

2. Three protection concepts and their complementarity

We separate three properties that the literature conflates.

Definition 1 (Material security). Participant $i$ is materially secure at time $t$ iff realized resources satisfy $\rho_i(t) \geq \rho_{\min}$. Material security is a fact about realized outcomes, not a contractual promise.

Definition 2 (Contractual protection). A protocol offers contractual protection at level $p_i \in [0,1]$ iff it specifies an enforceable obligation to compensate losses up to $p_i \cdot \rho_i(0)$ when realized resources fall below the threshold:
\text{comp}_i(t) = \max\left(0,\; p_i \rho_i(0) - \rho_i(t)\right).


Contractual protection is a legal-institutional commitment, not a guarantee of solvency.

Definition 3 (Transferable protection). A protection regime $p$ is transferable to target environment Ω at grade $g \in [0,1]^3$ iff the guarantee mechanism can be reproduced in Ω along three dimensions:
\mathcal{T}(p, \Pi, \Omega) = (g_{\text{fiscal}}, g_{\text{legal}}, g_{\text{admin}}) \in [0,1]^3,


where $g_{\text{fiscal}}$ measures the fraction of the funding mechanism reproducible under Ω's fiscal capacity, $g_{\text{legal}}$ the fraction enforceable under Ω's legal authority, and $g_{\text{admin}}$ the fraction deliverable under Ω's administrative infrastructure.

Remark 1 (Graded transferability). A binary coding of transferability is a substantive error: a mechanism can be 80% fiscally reproducible and 40% administratively reproducible, and the transfer error should reflect the grade, not the binary.

Remark 2 (Horizon). Transferability also has a horizon dimension. A 24-month temporary protection is not the same intervention as a permanent policy. We define the horizon ratio $h_{\Pi}/h_{\Omega}$ and treat it as a separate transfer dimension (Section 7).

Complementarity. The three concepts are complements, not separable dimensions. Contractual protection without material security is a paper promise. Material security without transferable protection produces evidence about an arrangement that cannot exist in Ω. Transferable protection without enforcement in Ω is a policy proposal without a delivery mechanism.

Definition 4 (Coherent protection regime — corrected). Version 0.3 defined coherence as the minimum of three incommensurable objects. This was mathematically undefined. We now define three normalized scores in $[0,1]$:
s_{\text{mat}} = \min\left(1,\; \frac{\rho_i(t)}{\rho_{\min}}\right), \qquad
s_{\text{con}} = p_i, \qquad
s_{\text{trans}} = \min(g_{\text{fiscal}}, g_{\text{legal}}, g_{\text{admin}}).


A protection regime is coherent at level $\kappa \in [0,1]$ iff
\min(s_{\text{mat}}, s_{\text{con}}, s_{\text{trans}}) \geq \kappa.


The coherence deficit is $1 - \kappa$. Coherence is the design object, not any single dimension.

Remark 3 (Why the scores are normalized). The normalization is a modelling choice, not a measurement. $s_{\text{mat}}$ treats material security as a ratio capped at one; $s_{\text{con}}$ inherits the contractual level; $s_{\text{trans}}$ uses the weakest transfer dimension. Alternatives (geometric mean, weighted sum) are possible and should be pre-registered. The important point is that the aggregate is defined on a common scale, so that the coherence deficit used in P3 is a well-defined quantity.

