Definition 5 (Protocol). A protocol is a tuple $\Pi = (\mathcal{N}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{Y}, p, \pi, V, \tau, \gamma, \phi)$, where $\pi$ is an assignment rule, $V$ a verification mechanism, $\tau$ a transfer mechanism, $\gamma$ a governance mechanism, and $\phi$ a portability mechanism.

4. Axioms and scope conditions

A1 (Budget). $\sum_i \text{comp}_i(t) + \text{admin}(p) + \text{admin}(\phi) + c_V \iota \leq B$ for all $t$.

A2 (Realized security). For all $i$ and $t$, $\rho_i(t) \geq \rho_{\min}$ whenever the protection mechanism is funded and enforced. A2 is a scope condition: protocols that do not satisfy it are outside the framework's domain.

A3 (Institutional separation). The verification body $V$ is not controlled by the designer $D$ in the dimensions reported by $\iota$.

A4 (Non-imposition). Exit is permitted at any $t$, with cost $c_i^{\text{exit}}(t)$ bounded as in Definition 6 below.

A5 (Common outcome function). For P3, we require $m_\Pi = m_\Omega = m$ unless the structural-violation term $\Lambda$ is invoked. We distinguish the common-function case (P3a) from the violation case (P3b) explicitly.

