6. Proposition P2 — Reversibility as a dynamic and three-component condition

6.1 Three portability components

Definition 8 (Portability decomposition). For each commitment type $j \in \{1,\ldots,k\}$:

· $\phi_j^{\text{formal}} \in \{0,1\}$: legal and technical right to portability exists;
· $\phi_j^{\text{effective}} \in [0,1]$: actual usability, measured as a function of time-to-exit and cost;
· $\phi_j^{\text{value}} \in [0,1]$: value ratio of the commitment before and after transfer.

The effective commitment after portability is
K_i^{\text{post}}(t) = \sum_j w_j K_{ij}(t)\left[1 - \phi_j^{\text{formal}}(t)\,\phi_j^{\text{effective}}(t)\,\phi_j^{\text{value}}(t)\right].

Definition 9 (Reversibility after portability — unified). The exit cost after portability is
c_i^{\text{exit}}(t) = \sum_j w_j K_{ij}(t)\left[1 - \phi_j(t)\right], \qquad \phi_j(t) := \phi_j^{\text{formal}}(t)\,\phi_j^{\text{effective}}(t)\,\phi_j^{\text{value}}(t).


$\Pi$ is $\epsilon$-reversible iff $c_i^{\text{exit}}(t) \leq \epsilon \rho_i(0)$ for all $i, t$.

Remark 6 (Notation unified). Version 0.3 used a scalar $\phi_i$ in Definition 6 and a vector $\phi_j$ in Definition 10. Version 0.4 uses the vector form throughout. The scalar aggregation $\phi_i = \sum_j w_j K_{ij} \phi_j / \sum_j w_j K_{ij}$ can be reported for summary purposes but is not the primitive object.

Remark 7 (Why the multiplicative form). Portability is only useful if all three conditions hold. A legal right that is administratively unusable ($\phi^{\text{effective}} = 0$) provides no exit. A usable mechanism that transfers a devalued commitment ($\phi^{\text{value}} = 0$) provides no exit either. The GDPR data-portability right illustrates both failures: it is formally available but often practically unusable due to format incompatibility and network effects, and its value is contested in data-driven markets.

6.2 The dynamic failure theorem

Proposition P2.1 (Dynamic reversibility failure — corrected). Suppose $K_{ij}(t)$ is non-decreasing in $t$ with $\dot{K}_{ij}(t) \geq k_j > 0$ for some $j$, $w_j > 0$, and $\phi_j(t) \equiv \phi_j < 1$ is constant. If $\sum_{j:\, \phi_j < 1} w_j(1-\phi_j)k_j > 0$, then for any $\epsilon > 0$ and any $\rho_i(0) < \infty$, there exists
t^* = \max\left(0,\; \frac{\epsilon \rho_i(0) - \sum_j w_j(1-\phi_j)K_{ij}(0)}{\sum_{j:\,\phi_j<1} w_j(1-\phi_j)k_j}\right)


such that $c_i^{\text{exit}}(t) > \epsilon \rho_i(0)$ for all $t > t^*$: $\epsilon$-reversibility fails eventually.

Proof. $c_i^{\text{exit}}(t) = \sum_j w_j K_{ij}(t)(1-\phi_j) \geq \sum_j w_j(1-\phi_j)(K_{ij}(0) + k_j t)$ for the growing types. If the denominator is positive, the right-hand side grows linearly with positive slope and crosses the threshold at the stated $t^*$. If the denominator is zero, either the initial cost already exceeds the threshold ($t^* = 0$) or all growing types have $\phi_j = 1$ and the failure does not arise from those types. $\square$

Remark 8 (Zero-denominator case). Version 0.3 did not handle the case $\sum w_j(1-\phi_j)k_j = 0$. This occurs when all growing commitment types are perfectly portable ($\phi_j = 1$) or have zero weight. In that case the linear growth argument does not apply; the exit cost may still grow through non-growing types, and the analysis must be conducted separately.

Corollary P2.2 (Four design levers, and their costs). To maintain $\epsilon$-reversibility indefinitely, the designer must choose at least one of: (i) $w_j = 0$ for all $j$ (no valuable commitments — defeats experimental depth); (ii) bound $K_{ij}(t) \leq \bar{K}_j$ (short horizon — limits the phenomena that can be studied); (iii) let $\phi_j(t) \to 1$ as $t \to \infty$ (perfect portability — requires building portability infrastructure); (iv) accept a finite reversibility window $[0, t^*]$. The four levers are not independent: increasing $\phi_j$ requires $\text{admin}(\phi_j)$ budget, which under A1 reduces the budget available for $p$ and $\iota$.

Remark 9 (Counting correction). Version 0.3 announced "three design levers" but listed four. The correct count is four. The first two (setting $w_j = 0$ and bounding $K_{ij}$) are distinct: the first eliminates the commitment, the second caps its accumulation.

Corollary P2.3 (Budget coupling). Under A1 with $\text{admin}'(\phi) > 0$, any increase in $\phi$ must be financed by a reduction in $p$, a reduction in $\iota$, or an administrative efficiency gain. This is a specific instance of the non-dominance problem (P4).

6.3 Portability audit protocol

The reversibility section of every protocol must report, for each commitment type $j$:

1. $\phi_j^{\text{formal}}$: legal/technical authority cited.
2. $\phi_j^{\text{effective}}$: time-to-exit (hours), cost (fraction of $\rho_i(0)$), receiving institution's recognition decision.
3. $\phi_j^{\text{value}}$: value ratio before/after transfer, with the valuation method specified in advance. For non-market commitments (reputation, data), the method must be named (e.g., replacement cost, hedonic estimate, stated preference) and its limitations stated.
4. $\text{admin}(\phi_j)$: administrative cost of the portability infrastructure.
5. Dynamic check: predicted $t^*$ under the observed $K$-growth rate, with the formula from Proposition P2.1, including the zero-denominator case.
6. Audit schedule: $t_1, \ldots, t_k$ at which the above are re-measured.

The audit must be performed by a party not controlled by the designer, and the results reported even if unfavorable.

