8. Proposition P4 — Non-dominance: restricted theorem and open problem

8.1 The restricted theorem

Theorem P4 (Binding-budget non-dominance). Consider experiments with budget $B$, costly protection $c_p$ per unit, and costly verification $c_V$ per unit. The feasible set is $\mathcal{P} = \{(p, \iota) \in [0,1]^2 : c_p p + c_V \iota \leq B\}$. If $B < c_p + c_V$, then no protocol achieves $(p, \iota) = (1,1)$, and the Pareto frontier is the line segment
\mathcal{F} = \{(p, \iota) : c_p p + c_V \iota = B,\; p \in [0, B/c_p],\; \iota \in [0, B/c_V]\},


which is non-degenerate.

Proof. $(1,1) \in \mathcal{P}$ iff $c_p + c_V \leq B$. If $B < c_p + c_V$, $(1,1) \notin \mathcal{P}$. On the line $c_p p + c_V \iota = B$ with $p, \iota > 0$, any increase in $p$ requires a decrease in $\iota$, so no point dominates another. Points strictly inside the budget set are dominated by boundary points. The segment has positive length because $B > 0$. $\square$

Corollary P4.1 (Four objectives — corrected). The theorem extends to four objectives $(p, \iota, \nu^{-1}, \text{reversibility})$ if the costs of all four are linear, $B$ is binding, all four costs are strictly positive, and no objective is perfectly correlated with another. Under these conditions the Pareto frontier is a simplex of dimension 3. If one objective is costless or two objectives are collinear, the effective dimension is lower.

Remark 13 (Triviality of the restricted theorem). The theorem establishes budgetary incompatibility, not structural incompatibility. Any two costly goods trade off under a binding budget. The informative question is whether the frontier persists when $B$ is large.

8.2 The open problem

Open problem P4. Characterize the class of experiments for which the Pareto frontier over $(p, \iota, \nu^{-1}, \text{reversibility})$ is non-degenerate even when $B \geq c_p + c_V + c_\nu + c_r$ — that is, when each objective can be individually maximized but not jointly. A structural conflict would arise if the portability mechanism required for reversibility necessarily reduced the material security available to participants through a non-budgetary channel (e.g., administrative attention, legal liability). No proof that such a conflict exists for any class is currently available, and no proof that it does not exist is available either.

8.3 Counterexample-search protocol

The protocol is specified here as a prospective procedure. No counterexample search has been executed for this version. The procedure is:

1. Specify a minimal class: two-period experiments, fixed budget, binary protection, binary verification, linear portability technology.
2. Compute the achievable set: enumerate the achievable $(p, \iota, \nu^{-1}, \text{rev})$ quadruples and identify the non-dominated set.
3. Check for a dominating protocol. If one exists, P4 is false for that class, and this is a substantive result.
4. If no dominating protocol exists, identify which pair of objectives conflicts and whether the conflict is budgetary (relaxable by efficiency gains) or structural (not relaxable within the class).
5. Report honestly. A counterexample to P4 for a well-specified class is more valuable than a conjecture that the paper itself seems ambivalent about.

Until the open problem is resolved, P4 is cited in later chapters only as a hypothesis, never as a design constraint.

