Appendix C — Proof obligations for P3

C.1 Lipschitz constants

The decomposition requires $L_p, \kappa, \gamma, L_\rho$. None is estimated from the protocol's data alone. The protocol must report for each:

· the range used (low, central, high);
· the source of the range (auxiliary study, theoretical bound, or judgment);
· the sensitivity of $\nu$ to the range.

If a range cannot be justified, the corresponding component is reported as unmeasured, not as bounded.

C.2 Wasserstein estimation

$\mathcal{W}_1(F_\Pi, F_\Omega)$ can be computed exactly for low-dimensional $\rho$. For high-dimensional $\rho$, entropic regularization is required, and the regularization parameter must be reported. The protocol must state:

· the dimension of $\rho$;
· the estimator used (exact, entropic, or other);
· the regularization parameter, if applicable;
· the sensitivity of the estimate to the regularization.

A Wasserstein estimate reported without these is not reproducible.

C.3 $\delta_{\text{site}}$ as conditional residual

$\delta_{\text{site}}$ is defined as the divergence of conditional effects after reweighting:

\delta_{\text{site}} := \sup_{\rho} \left| \mathbb{E}[m_\Omega \mid \rho, \text{site}=\text{eval}] - \mathbb{E}[m_\Omega \mid \rho, \text{site}=\text{target}] \right|.

If it were defined as a population divergence, it would double-count the Wasserstein term, because $F_\Pi$ and $F_\Omega$ already differ in composition and $\mathcal{W}_1$ captures that difference. The conditional definition isolates the site-specific heterogeneity that remains after composition is accounted for.

Requirement. The protocol must report:

· the reweighting method (e.g., inverse propensity weighting, calibration, matching);
· the comparison sites used;
· the covariates on which conditional effects are compared;
· the uncertainty in the residual, where it can be estimated.

If no comparison sites exist, $\delta_{\text{site}}$ is reported as unmeasured.

C.4 $\Lambda$ versus the Ben-David joint ideal error

Version 0.3 used language that conflated two distinct quantities.

· $\Lambda$ in this framework is $\sup_\rho |m_\Pi(\rho, p_\Omega) - m_\Omega(\rho, p_\Omega)|$: the maximum discrepancy between the two structural outcome functions at the same protection level. It asks how different the structures are.
· The Ben-David $\lambda$ is the minimum joint error achievable by a hypothesis on both domains. It asks how well a single model can fit both.

The two answer different questions. $\Lambda$ enters the decomposition because it bounds $|b-c|$; $\lambda$ does not. A paper that reports $\Lambda$ but calls it $\lambda$ (or vice versa) is making a category error. The protocol must use the $\Lambda$ definition and state that it is not the domain-adaptation joint error.

C.5 Status flags and reporting template

Every component of the six-component table must carry a status flag. The template is:

Component Status Value / scenario Source
Protection divergence bounded / estimated / unmeasured ... $L_p$ range; $\|p_\Pi - p_\Omega\|_\infty$
Structural violation unmeasured (default) named scenario $\Lambda$ scenarios
Horizon divergence bounded / estimated / unmeasured ... $\kappa$ range; $\|h_\Pi - h_\Omega\|$
Scale divergence bounded / estimated / unmeasured ... $\gamma$; $\|s_\Pi - s_\Omega\|$
Composition bounded / estimated / unmeasured ... $\mathcal{W}_1$ estimate
Site-selection residual bounded / estimated / unmeasured ... conditional residual

A table in which every row is "unmeasured" is a valid output. It says the transfer is not identified. A table in which an unmeasured row is presented as a number is a misreport.

