Appendix D — P4 counterexample search: specification (not executed)

Status: not executed. No counterexample search has been run for this version. The specification below is the procedure that would be followed.

D.1 Minimal class specification

· Two-period experiments ($t = 0, 1$).
· Fixed budget $B$.
· Binary protection level $p \in \{0, 1\}$.
· Binary verification design $\iota \in \{0, 1\}$.
· Linear portability technology: $\phi$ costs $c_\phi$ per unit and yields exit cost reduction proportional to $\phi$.
· Objectives: $(p, \iota, \nu^{-1}, \text{rev})$.

D.2 Enumeration procedure

1. Discretize the technology parameters ($c_p, c_V, c_\phi$) on a grid.
2. For each parameter combination, enumerate the achievable quadruples $(p, \iota, \nu^{-1}, \text{rev})$.
3. Identify the non-dominated set.
4. Record the dimension of the frontier and the identity of the objectives that trade off.

D.3 Dominance criteria

A quadruple $x$ dominates $y$ iff $x \geq y$ componentwise and $x > y$ in at least one component. The frontier is the set of non-dominated quadruples.

D.4 Reporting template

Parameter combination Frontier dimension Trading objectives Budgetary or structural conflict
... ... ... ...

D.5 What would count as a substantive result

· A dominating protocol (frontier collapses to a point) for a well-specified class: P4 is false for that class.
· A structural conflict (frontier non-degenerate even when $B$ is large) for a well-specified class: P4 is true for that class, and the mechanism should be identified.
· No structural conflict across the grid: P4 is a budgetary phenomenon only, and should be cited as such.

Until the search is executed, P4 is cited as a hypothesis, never as a design constraint.

