Appendix E — Normalization and aggregation choices for Definition 4

E.1 The three scores

s_{\text{mat}} = \min\left(1,\; \frac{\rho_i(t)}{\rho_{\min}}\right), \qquad
s_{\text{con}} = p_i, \qquad
s_{\text{trans}} = \min(g_{\text{fiscal}}, g_{\text{legal}}, g_{\text{admin}}).

E.2 Aggregation alternatives

Aggregator Formula Property
Minimum $\min(s_{\text{mat}}, s_{\text{con}}, s_{\text{trans}})$ Coherence requires all three to be high; conservative
Geometric mean $(s_{\text{mat}} s_{\text{con}} s_{\text{trans}})^{1/3}$ Allows substitution; less conservative
Weighted sum $w_1 s_{\text{mat}} + w_2 s_{\text{con}} + w_3 s_{\text{trans}}$ Requires normative weights; not conservative

E.3 Why the minimum is the default

The three components are complements, not substitutes. A regime with perfect material security but no contractual protection is a paper promise; a regime with perfect contractual protection but no transferable mechanism produces evidence about an arrangement that cannot exist in Ω. The minimum captures the complementarity: the coherence level is set by the weakest component. This is a modelling choice, not a measurement, and it must be stated as such.

E.4 Sensitivity requirement

The protocol must report the coherence level under at least two aggregators (minimum and geometric mean) and state whether the ranking of candidate regimes changes. If the ranking changes, the coherence claim is reported as aggregator-dependent.

