Appendix I — Negative-finding clause template for L4

I.1 Purpose

The retrospective behavioural test (has $V$ ever published a negative finding without retaliation?) is necessary but not sufficient: a body that has never had a negative finding to publish may still be independent. The prospective clause converts the test into a design feature.

I.2 Template

The verification body $V$ commits, before data collection, to publish at least one pre-specified negative result if the data meet the pre-specified criterion for a negative finding. The criterion is: [state the criterion]. The budget and appointment protection for $V$ are: [state the protections]. If $V$ does not publish a negative finding and the criterion is met, the protocol reports L4 as failed, regardless of the $\iota$ vector.

I.3 What the clause does not do

· It does not require $V$ to manufacture a negative finding. If the data do not meet the criterion, $V$ publishes a null or positive finding, and L4 is assessed on the $\iota$ vector and the historical record.
· It does not guarantee independence. It makes the appearance of independence harder to sustain without substance.
· It does not substitute for the $\iota$ vector. It supplements it.

I.4 Reporting

The clause and its outcome are reported in the verification section of the protocol. A protocol without the clause is reported as having retrospective-only verification independence.

