Appendix K — Scope and status statement

K.1 What has been done

· Formal statements and proofs of P1, P2, and the restricted P4.
· A decomposition (not a bound) of transfer error into six components.
· An architecture of six layers with indicators and conventions.
· Decision rules F1–F4 with comparators and thresholds.
· A calibration protocol specified prospectively.
· Proof obligations and templates for each component.

K.2 What has not been done

· No experiments have been conducted.
· No physical tests have been conducted.
· No coding exercises or training have been conducted.
· No calibration has been executed.
· No counterexample search for P4 has been executed.
· No data have been collected.
· No empirical estimate of $\nu$, $\Lambda$, $\delta_{\text{site}}$, or any other component has been produced.
· No verification of the Vivalt attribution or any other numerical anchor has been completed beyond what is stated in the main text.

K.3 What the framework can and cannot claim

The framework can claim:

· a well-defined coherence concept after the Definition 4 correction;
· a decomposition that makes the components of transfer error visible;
· a set of proof obligations that any application must meet;
· a reporting discipline that makes a false transfer claim harder to make.

The framework cannot claim:

· that the transfer error is bounded in any application where $\Lambda$ or $\delta_{\text{site}}$ is unmeasured;
· that the signability condition is established by the graph alone;
· that the architecture has been validated empirically;
· that P4 is resolved beyond the binding-budget case;
· that any numerical threshold in the architecture is derived from evidence rather than declared as a convention.

The framework's contribution is the reporting discipline. The formal results are in service of that discipline, not a substitute for it.

